WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:09.449
That's one of the most important

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:11.429
lessons in Jacob Mchangama's new

00:00:11.430 --> 00:00:13.679
book "Free Speech: A History

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:15.309
From Socrates to Social Media."

00:00:15.310 --> 00:00:17.189
Mchangama heads up the Danish

00:00:17.190 --> 00:00:18.509
think tank Justicia.

00:00:18.510 --> 00:00:20.129
He's worried about a proposal that

00:00:20.130 --> 00:00:21.599
would make hate speech a crime under

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:23.129
European Union law and give

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:25.259
bureaucrats in Brussels sweeping

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:27.239
powers to prosecute people spewing

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:28.769
venom at religious and ethnic

00:00:28.770 --> 00:00:31.229
minorities. Members of the LGBT

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:33.479
plus community, women and others.

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:35.309
Europe's history with such laws

00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:36.489
argues against them.

00:00:36.490 --> 00:00:38.639
In the 1920s, Germany's viremia

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:40.469
republic strictly regulated

00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:42.059
the press and invoked emergency

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:44.369
powers to crack down on Nazi speech.

00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:45.899
It censored and prosecuted the

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:47.639
editor of the anti-Semitic Nazi

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:48.640
paper Der Sturmer Julius Streicher,

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:51.899
who used his trial as a platform

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:53.399
for spreading his views and his

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:54.959
imprisonment as a way of turning

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:57.209
himself into a martyr and his cause

00:00:57.210 --> 00:00:58.499
into a crusade.

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:00.029
When the Nazis took power in the

00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:01.739
early thirties, they expanded

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:03.569
existing laws and precedents

00:01:03.570 --> 00:01:05.429
to shut down dissent and freedom of

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:07.439
assembly. Contemporary scholarship

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:08.459
suggests that there can be a

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:10.499
backlash effect when government shut

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:12.299
down speech, leading otherwise

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:14.279
moderate people to embrace fringe

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:16.109
beliefs. Mchangama points

00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:18.299
to a 2017 study published

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:19.799
in the European Journal of Political

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:21.779
Research that concluded extremism

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:23.789
in Western Europe was fueled in part

00:01:23.790 --> 00:01:26.279
by extensive public repression

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:28.079
of radical right actors and

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:29.009
opinions.

00:01:29.010 --> 00:01:31.229
In 1965, the United Kingdom

00:01:31.230 --> 00:01:33.209
passed a law banning incitement

00:01:33.210 --> 00:01:34.499
to racial hatred.

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:36.089
But one of the very first people

00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:38.159
prosecuted under it was a black

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:40.019
Briton who called whites vicious

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:41.939
and nasty people in a speech.

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:43.769
More recently, Mchangama notes that

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:45.629
radical feminists in England

00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:47.039
have been charged with offending

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:49.229
LGBT+ people because

00:01:49.230 --> 00:01:50.969
they insist there are biological

00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:52.739
differences between the sexes.

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:55.289
In France and LGBT+

00:01:55.290 --> 00:01:57.179
rights organization was fined for

00:01:57.180 --> 00:01:58.769
calling an opponent of same sex

00:01:58.770 --> 00:02:00.239
marriage a homophobe.

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:02.129
Once the principle of free speech is

00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:03.130
abandoned, warns Mchangama, any

00:02:04.470 --> 00:02:06.269
minority can end up being targeted

00:02:06.270 --> 00:02:08.129
rather than protected by laws

00:02:08.130 --> 00:02:09.809
against hatred and offense.

00:02:09.810 --> 00:02:11.339
That's what happened in Canada in

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:13.409
the 1990s, after the Supreme Court

00:02:13.410 --> 00:02:15.299
there ruled that words and images

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:17.399
that degrade women could be banned.

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:19.169
The decision was based in part on

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:20.549
the legal theories of Andrea

00:02:20.550 --> 00:02:22.229
Dworkin, whose books on why

00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:24.179
pornography should be banned were

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:26.309
briefly seized by Canadian customs

00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:28.139
agents under the laws she

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:29.609
helped to inspire.

00:02:29.610 --> 00:02:30.899
First Amendment rights are still

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:32.729
very popular in the United States,

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:34.559
with 91 percent of us in

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:36.419
a recent survey agreeing that

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:37.919
protecting free speech is an

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:39.749
important part of American

00:02:39.750 --> 00:02:40.559
democracy.

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:42.389
But fully 60 percent of us

00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:43.589
said that the government should

00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:45.539
prohibit people from sharing of

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:47.639
racist or bigoted idea, hearing

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:49.589
hateful words and ideas, outrages

00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:51.329
and discomforts most of us.

00:02:51.330 --> 00:02:53.069
But Mission Gama's history of free

00:02:53.070 --> 00:02:54.959
speech underscores that state

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:56.789
suppression can grant those

00:02:56.790 --> 00:02:58.289
words and ideas more power and

00:02:58.290 --> 00:03:00.179
influence, and that the best

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:02.069
antidote to hate in a free and

00:03:02.070 --> 00:03:04.169
open society is not to hide

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:05.969
from it, but to loudly and

00:03:05.970 --> 00:03:07.860
persuasively confront it.

